      },
      "line-43": {
        "id": "line-43",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "Any realistic romance movie nowadays would mostly be about people texting.",
        "end": true
      }
    }
  },
  "tweet_2020312874024071343_1": {
    "id": "tweet_2020312874024071343_1",
    "title": "When I was a child, I thought that landfills were terrible.",
    "source_directory": "source-tweets",
    "source_files": ["2020312874024071343.txt"],
    "start": "line-1",
    "nodes": {
      "line-1": {
        "id": "line-1",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "When I was a child, I thought that landfills were terrible.",
        "next": "line-2"
      },
      "line-2": {
        "id": "line-2",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Just dumping everything in one spot seems so messy and contrary to the legibility and order that we think of as good.",
        "next": "line-3"
      },
      "line-3": {
        "id": "line-3",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "Not to mention the environment: isn't it bad just to put all the garbage in the ground?",
        "next": "line-4"
      },
      "line-4": {
        "id": "line-4",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "In my childish mind, everything should have been cleanly sorted for recycling. It seemed so dumb to just dump it on one big pile on the ground.",
        "next": "line-5"
      },
      "line-5": {
        "id": "line-5",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Now it turns out that this is probably optimal.",
        "next": "line-6"
      },
      "line-6": {
        "id": "line-6",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "Right. We've learned that most \"recycling\" is a scam, where garbage gets sold and resold between recycling venues until finally it ends up with somebody who just dumps it into the Pacific ocean.",
        "next": "line-7"
      },
      "line-7": {
        "id": "line-7",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "On the other hand, putting stuff in a landfill is cheap and scalable.",
        "next": "line-8"
      },
      "line-8": {
        "id": "line-8",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "It's easy to fit a lot of stuff in a landfill, and ensure that it doesn't spread everywhere -- when you're done, you can cover it.",
        "next": "line-9"
      },
      "line-9": {
        "id": "line-9",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "What's most interesting is that a landfill is fully recoverable.",
        "next": "line-10"
      },
      "line-10": {
        "id": "line-10",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "When there's any kind of materials shortage, you can in fact just go to the landfill and start mining it, which has started happening over the last couple of years.",
        "next": "line-11"
      },
      "line-11": {
        "id": "line-11",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "Enterprising governments/people are mining their landfills for rare earth metals, and it's working.",
        "next": "line-12"
      },
      "line-12": {
        "id": "line-12",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Which really just makes the landfill a kind of a temporary deposit structure before eventually recycling or reusing those materials in a more efficient way.",
        "next": "line-13"
      },
      "line-13": {
        "id": "line-13",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "That seems pretty good!",
        "next": "line-14"
      },
      "line-14": {
        "id": "line-14",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Maybe this is one of those \"worse is better\" cases.",
        "end": true
      }
    }
  },
  "tweet_2020975269792841866_1": {
    "id": "tweet_2020975269792841866_1",
    "title": "Unless we hit the software singularity, the way AI-enabled engineering is goi...",
    "source_directory": "source-tweets",
    "source_files": ["2020975269792841866.txt"],
    "start": "line-1",
    "nodes": {
      "line-1": {
        "id": "line-1",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Unless we hit the software singularity, the way AI-enabled engineering is going to play out is something like this:",
        "next": "line-2"
      },
      "line-2": {
        "id": "line-2",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "1. Codebases are going to 10-100x in size and complexity, thanks to the AI's prolific ability to write code. Why? Most codebases are wildly under-built, with endless bugs to fix and backlogs of features to add.",
        "next": "line-3"
      },
      "line-3": {
        "id": "line-3",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "2. The size/complexity expansion eventually hits some scaling limit in size/taste -- not for fault of the AI, but for fault of the engineer's ability to correctly specify *what they want* initially -- and then engineering skill + intuition matters again, just at a much larger scale of codebase/abstraction.",
        "next": "line-4"
      },
      "line-4": {
        "id": "line-4",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "3. Right now is a weird spot in the engineering meta where people are having AI write 1x-size codebases and saying \"Wow this is so easy, totally different skill set\" but not realizing that most codebases are bad. 1x is not the benchmark for work completion: 10x is the benchmark now.",
        "next": "line-5"
      },
      "line-5": {
        "id": "line-5",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "4. Gaining leverage is the story of software engineering over the last sixty years. I can write in Python in twenty minutes what would take me two weeks in pure C. AI adds a similar leverage multiplier.",
        "end": true
      }
    }
  },
  "tweet_2022881084585943524_1": {
    "id": "tweet_2022881084585943524_1",
    "title": "I moved to Silicon Valley when I was 23. Like many other people in my cohort,...",
    "source_directory": "source-tweets",
    "source_files": ["2022881084585943524.txt"],
    "start": "line-1",
    "nodes": {
      "line-1": {
        "id": "line-1",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "I moved to Silicon Valley when I was 23. Like many other people in my cohort, I helped out on some startups and got some equity.",
        "next": "line-2"
      },
      "line-2": {
        "id": "line-2",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "I carefully thought about how much that equity might be worth one day. Maybe a million dollars? A life-changing amount of money for a 23-year-old.",
        "next": "line-3"
      },
      "line-3": {
        "id": "line-3",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Almost ten years later, some of this equity has appreciated a lot, but it's still not liquid.",
        "next": "line-4"
      },
      "line-4": {
        "id": "line-4",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Even if it were to be liquid soon, it's a far cry from what I imagined: at 33, and after nine years of inflation, a million dollars isn't a life-changing amount of money anymore.",
        "next": "line-5"
      },
      "line-5": {
        "id": "line-5",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "At 23, this money would've bought a lot of joy and opportunity -- but at 33, that time and situation has, to some extent, passed. They say that youth is wasted on the young and money is wasted on the old.",
        "next": "line-6"
      },
      "line-6": {
        "id": "line-6",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "Perhaps your time preference should be shorter if the assets are compounding on someone else's terms, and longer if they're compounding on your terms.",
        "end": true
      }
    }
  },
