  },
  "tweet_2035055266434359437_1": {
    "id": "tweet_2035055266434359437_1",
    "title": "I frequently ask engineering candidates what the difference between a git reb...",
    "source_directory": "source-tweets",
    "source_files": ["2035055266434359437.txt"],
    "start": "line-1",
    "nodes": {
      "line-1": {
        "id": "line-1",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "I frequently ask engineering candidates what the difference between a git rebase and a git merge is.",
        "next": "line-2"
      },
      "line-2": {
        "id": "line-2",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "I have heard so many (100+) garbled, nonsensical, or subtly wrong answers -- but always using the right vocab -- over the past few months, that at this point I feel totally fried and would not personally answer this question as well as I would have before I started doing this.",
        "next": "line-3"
      },
      "line-3": {
        "id": "line-3",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "I frequently have to look it up just to override all the wrong stuff I am hearing.",
        "next": "line-4"
      },
      "line-4": {
        "id": "line-4",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "This is like context window pollution for LLMs!",
        "end": true
      }
    }
  },
  "tweet_2035229317756809673_1": {
    "id": "tweet_2035229317756809673_1",
    "title": "What have you been thinking about lately?",
    "source_directory": "source-tweets",
    "source_files": ["2035229317756809673.txt"],
    "start": "line-1",
    "nodes": {
      "line-1": {
        "id": "line-1",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "What have you been thinking about lately?",
        "next": "line-2"
      },
      "line-2": {
        "id": "line-2",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "The following has been on my mind since I first encountered it almost 15 years ago.",
        "next": "line-3"
      },
      "line-3": {
        "id": "line-3",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "When I was in high school, I had a few teachers in the humanities/social sciences who were really, really good: deep, serious thinkers, with lots of interesting views synthesized over decades of globetrotting experiences.",
        "next": "line-4"
      },
      "line-4": {
        "id": "line-4",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "As teachers, even at a nice private school, they were not real \"winners\" in the sense of climbing a prestigious career ladder, and neither did they publish academic papers.",
        "next": "line-5"
      },
      "line-5": {
        "id": "line-5",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "You could call them very advanced amateurs, and as dabblers they got to toy with lots of interesting ideas, kind of randomly assembled, without outside judgment.",
        "next": "line-6"
      },
      "line-6": {
        "id": "line-6",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "When I got to the University of Chicago, known for its life of the mind in the humanities, I didn't really find anybody who seemed to be as deep or as interesting a thinker as these teachers I encountered in high school.",
        "next": "line-7"
      },
      "line-7": {
        "id": "line-7",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "Why do you think that is?",
        "next": "line-8"
      },
      "line-8": {
        "id": "line-8",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Partially, it's because I got lucky with my teachers. They were the best we had. Maybe I didn't get so lucky with my professors.",
        "next": "line-9"
      },
      "line-9": {
        "id": "line-9",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "But today I may have figured it out: I think the actual reason is that my professors at UChicago were, in a sense, winners on an academic career ladder.",
        "next": "line-10"
      },
      "line-10": {
        "id": "line-10",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "It's an extremely competitive environment, and they had somehow made it to the top. By definition, this is a tremendously powerful filter.",
        "next": "line-11"
      },
      "line-11": {
        "id": "line-11",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "And I think this had actually filtered against a whole group of people whom I consider interesting.",
        "next": "line-12"
      },
      "line-12": {
        "id": "line-12",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "This has become especially clear over the last few years, as a lot of traditional academia has been losing prestige rapidly: people are trusting the kind of professorial expert class less and less and less.",
        "next": "line-13"
      },
      "line-13": {
        "id": "line-13",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "It turned out that professors of ethics and sociology are just as unethical and susceptible to groupthink as the general public.",
        "next": "line-14"
      },
      "line-14": {
        "id": "line-14",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "And the general conformity of ideology and thought in academia is now well-known.",
        "next": "line-15"
      },
      "line-15": {
        "id": "line-15",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "These professional humanities academics may publish papers that are respected or even highly esteemed within their own niche communities, but this particular value system has long since been removed from what I consider interesting, or, in many cases, even related to the pursuit of truth.",
        "next": "line-16"
      },
      "line-16": {
        "id": "line-16",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Reflecting on it, the heart of the matter is that those teachers in high school were unconstrained by convention and had been allowed to fully lean into their interests --  kind of like the platonic dream of academia...",
        "next": "line-17"
      },
      "line-17": {
        "id": "line-17",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Whereas the professors I encountered in university, even when very successful, had been conformed by the academia-industry pressure cooker and their work sanitized, professionalized, and ultimately made uninteresting under the constraints.",
        "end": true
      }
    }
  },
  "tweet_2036531640676262187_1": {
    "id": "tweet_2036531640676262187_1",
    "title": "Given the PyPI supply chain attack, I recommend keeping a canary in the coalm...",
    "source_directory": "source-tweets",
    "source_files": ["2036531640676262187.txt"],
    "start": "line-1",
    "nodes": {
      "line-1": {
        "id": "line-1",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Given the PyPI supply chain attack, I recommend keeping a canary in the coalmine...",
        "next": "line-2"
      },
      "line-2": {
        "id": "line-2",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "I have a bitcoin private key containing $100 of BTC in my .bashrc. It's clearly labelled.",
        "next": "line-3"
      },
      "line-3": {
        "id": "line-3",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "If my system is ever compromised by some bad package, the BTC will get stolen, and I'll see the move on-chain. And that'll tell me that I need to rotate every single other secret.",
        "next": "line-4"
      },
      "line-4": {
        "id": "line-4",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "There are even services that will send you an alert (text, email, whatsapp...) if a given bitcoin address moves funds. It's good to have a burglar alarm, especially when time is of the essence.",
        "next": "line-5"
      },
      "line-5": {
        "id": "line-5",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "If you label it too obviously, won't a careful attacker just skip it on purpose to stay quiet longer?",
        "next": "line-6"
      },
      "line-6": {
        "id": "line-6",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Most attackers are not going to play that level of 3D chess, especially if the key has $100+ on it.",
        "end": true
      }
    }
  },
