  "tweet_2046071681530442156_1": {
    "id": "tweet_2046071681530442156_1",
    "title": "There's an interesting pricing issue where the all-in cost of something is of...",
    "source_directory": "source-tweets",
    "source_files": ["2046071681530442156.txt"],
    "start": "line-1",
    "nodes": {
      "line-1": {
        "id": "line-1",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "There's an interesting pricing issue where the all-in cost of something is often much more than the nominal cost of it, and so the nominal thing could actually be priced much higher, because the demand is so inelastic.",
        "next": "line-2"
      },
      "line-2": {
        "id": "line-2",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "For example. Say I pay $10 to go to an event, but I pay $20 for an Uber there, and $20 for an Uber back. My all-in cost is $50. They could've made the event 50% or 100% more expensive and it would've barely moved the needle.",
        "next": "line-3"
      },
      "line-3": {
        "id": "line-3",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "You would've easily paid.",
        "next": "line-4"
      },
      "line-4": {
        "id": "line-4",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Or suppose I'm going to a restaurant, but I'm taking a Waymo there vs. walking there. One of the two is $60 more expensive than the other, but it has no impact on the actual meal. If the restaurant knew, they could price that in.",
        "next": "line-5"
      },
      "line-5": {
        "id": "line-5",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "This reality is most revealed on DoorDash: the meal itself might cost only $20. But you're willing to pay another $15 to have it delivered. If you're willing to pay that, you're probably also willing to pay much more for the meal.",
        "next": "line-6"
      },
      "line-6": {
        "id": "line-6",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "This is why some DoorDash restaurants are absurdly expensive -- like $30 for a normal Indian curry -- because they've observed that the price of the dish doesn't really matter.",
        "next": "line-7"
      },
      "line-7": {
        "id": "line-7",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "Somehow the customer is already in a weird price-insensitive situation. They'll pay anything! They'll pay it on installments! (Really.)",
        "next": "line-8"
      },
      "line-8": {
        "id": "line-8",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "This is a great insight and most consumer businesses don't grasp it well enough.",
        "next": "line-9"
      },
      "line-9": {
        "id": "line-9",
        "speaker": "npc",
        "text": "I can't believe are clapping for \"DoorDash is expensive because people will pay for it\" and yet my multi-month gestation of \"We seem to be on a billion-plus year old trend of increasing compute per second per unit matter\" like a religious epiphany, gets crickets.",
        "next": "line-10"
      },
      "line-10": {
        "id": "line-10",
        "speaker": "player",
        "text": "You never know what hits.",
        "end": true
      }
    }
  },
  "tweet_2048490877056888951_1": {
    "id": "tweet_2048490877056888951_1",
    "title": "I once was at a casual restaurant where I asked the waiter for ketchup.",
